Keep the C library's isnormal and iszero

The __linux__ branch of System/Common/runtime.h defines

    #define iszero(f) ((f)==0.0)
    #define isnormal(f) ((int)1)  /* these two are hacks esc*/

but every generated C file includes <math.h> first, through c_header.h,
and <math.h> has defined isnormal since C99 and iszero since C23.
Redefining a standard macro is undefined behaviour. In practice the
later definition wins, so FLTD::is_normal, which Library/Base/fltd.config
compiles to isnormal($0), was true for every value: zero, the infinities
and NaN included. The C compiler also warned that isnormal was
redefined in every C file it compiled, which is most of what a student
sees when their code fails in the C compiler or the linker.

Define each stand-in only where the C library has not defined the name.
With glibc, isnormal is now the real classification. iszero is only
defined by <math.h> under C23 or _GNU_SOURCE; either way it means the
same as the stand-in.

issubnormal is not a standard name (C99 spells it fpclassify(x) ==
FP_SUBNORMAL), so its stand-in is left as it is, and FLTD::is_subnormal
is still always false. A real one needs to evaluate its argument once,
and the generated code can pass it a call expression, so it is not part
of this fix.

--- a/System/Common/runtime.h
+++ b/System/Common/runtime.h
@@ -174,9 +174,22 @@
 #ifdef __linux__
 # define FLTDEXP10(f)    pow(10.0,f)
                          /* Change 990218, by Nobbi: pow10 not available */
-#define iszero(f) ((f)==0.0)
+/* c_header.h includes <math.h> before this file, and <math.h> defines
+** isnormal from C99 on and iszero from C23 on. Redefining a standard
+** macro is undefined behaviour: in practice the definition here won, so
+** FLTD::is_normal was true for every value, zero, the infinities and NaN
+** included, and every generated C file drew a "redefined" warning. Keep
+** the C library's macro where there is one, and the stand-in otherwise.
+** issubnormal is not a standard name, so its stand-in is still used and
+** FLTD::is_subnormal is still always false.
+*/
+#ifndef iszero
+# define iszero(f) ((f)==0.0)
+#endif
 /* static int ilogb(double f) { int i; frexp(f,&i); return i-1; } */
-#define isnormal(f) ((int)1)  /* these two are hacks esc*/
+#ifndef isnormal
+# define isnormal(f) ((int)1)  /* these two are hacks esc*/
+#endif
 #define issubnormal(f) ((int)0)
 
 #elif defined(_AIX) || defined(SUNOS5) || defined(__NeXT__) || defined(__sgi) || defined(ALPHA) || defined(__hpux) || defined(__FreeBSD__) || defined(__alpha) || defined(__CYGWIN32__)
